I agents for the real world", "url": "https://sierra.ai/blog/benchmarking-ai-agents", "publisher": "Sierra"}, {"title": "τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07982", "publisher": "Barres et al. (arXiv; ICML 2026)", "date": "2025"}, {"title": "τ²-Bench (PMLR volume 306)", "url": "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/barres26a.html", "publisher": "Proceedings of Machine Learning Research", "date": "2026"}]}-->
<p>Many agent evaluations report a single number: "the agent completes 80% of tasks." That number usually comes from running each task once, or from the best of several runs. For a chatbot answering questions, it might be enough. For an agent that changes bookings, issues refunds or updates records, it hides the question that matters: if a customer asks for the same thing ten times, how often does the agent get it right every time?</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What τ-bench measures</h2>
<p>Sierra's τ-bench put agents in realistic customer service domains. The agent talks to a simulated user, calls domain APIs and must follow a written policy. Success is judged objectively: does the final state of the database match the expected outcome<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>?</p>
<p>It also introduced <strong>pass^k</strong>: the probability that an agent succeeds on all k independent attempts at the same task<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. That is a reliability measure, unlike pass@k, which asks whether any attempt succeeds. The authors reported that even state-of-the-art function-calling agents at the time succeeded on fewer than half the tasks, and that consistency was worse, with pass^8 below 25% in the retail domain<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>A 60% success rate on one attempt can mean a much lower chance of handling the same request correctly every time. Customers experience the second number.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>When users act too</h2>
<p>Real support often involves the customer doing things: restarting a device, checking a setting, confirming a code. τ²-bench extended the idea to a dual-control setting where both agent and user use tools to change a shared environment, and reported that performance dropped noticeably when agents moved from no-user settings to dual control<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Guiding a person through a task is a skill of its own, and it needs its own tests.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I build agent evaluations</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Judge outcomes, not transcripts.</strong> Wherever possible, check the end state in the system of record, as τ-bench does, rather than asking a model whether the conversation looked right.</li>
<li><strong>Write policies down, then test them.</strong> Refund limits, identity checks and escalation rules become explicit test cases, including ones where the right answer is "no".</li>
<li><strong>Simulate users with variety</strong>: impatient, confused, adversarial, and users who change their minds. Include prompt injection attempts, as in <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Run every task several times</strong> and report pass^k alongside the single-run rate. Release gates should use the reliability number.</li>
<li><strong>Grow the suite from production.</strong> Every incident and escalation becomes a test case, per <a href="/insights/ai-incident-postmortems/">AI incident postmortems</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Trace everything.</strong> Tool calls, arguments and results, so a failing run can be diagnosed; see <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>This sits on top of the general method in <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a>, with two additions specific to agents: state-based judging and repeated trials.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to do with the results</h2>
<p>Low reliability isn't always a reason to stop. It's a reason to change the design: narrower tools, deterministic code for policy checks, human approval for irreversible actions, and the containment patterns in <a href="/insights/mcp-apps-tool-annotations/">MCP Apps and tool annotations</a>. Often the best improvement isn't a better model but less for the model to decide.</p>
</section>

